1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,969
Two engineers sit next to each other.
Both have one local commit on main.

2
00:00:09,009 --> 00:00:13,230
Both push. One gets "fetch first".
The other gets "non-fast-forward".

3
00:00:13,270 --> 00:00:17,121
They are in the same situation:
a colleague pushed before them.

4
00:00:17,462 --> 00:00:23,951
Why two messages for one cause? And here
is the question that tells you whether

5
00:00:23,991 --> 00:00:28,332
someone understands push:
in both cases, who said no?

6
00:00:28,372 --> 00:00:34,944
Their own Git, or the server? The answer
is on the screen, in one word, and most

7
00:00:34,984 --> 00:00:37,093
people have never read it.

8
00:00:38,933 --> 00:00:42,536
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

9
00:00:42,576 --> 00:00:45,582
Fetch reads another repository.
Push writes to one.

10
00:00:45,622 --> 00:00:50,420
That makes push the first command in this
module that can change something you do

11
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:54,182
not own, and so it's the first
where another party gets a vote.

12
00:00:54,522 --> 00:00:59,517
Today you follow one push through its seven
steps and see where it can be refused,

13
00:00:59,557 --> 00:01:04,674
by whom, and with which words. Then you push
the things that are not branches of the

14
00:01:04,714 --> 00:01:07,928
ordinary kind: deletions,
tags, several refs at once.

15
00:01:07,968 --> 00:01:11,550
You look at pushes that fail
before any comparison is made.

16
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:14,804
You look at the rules a plain
Git server can enforce.

17
00:01:14,844 --> 00:01:19,900
And you close with section 12.9: why Git
refuses a push into a branch that somebody

18
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:20,882
has checked out.

19
00:01:21,222 --> 00:01:25,645
The server in the demos is a bare repository
on disk, which means it has no working

20
00:01:25,685 --> 00:01:30,107
tree, only the Git data. Where GitHub changes
the picture, the label on screen says

21
00:01:30,147 --> 00:01:30,269
so.

22
00:01:32,109 --> 00:01:33,687
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:34,027 --> 00:01:35,439
After this video you can:

24
00:01:35,779 --> 00:01:39,531
Describe a push as a request that
the receiving repository may refuse.

25
00:01:39,871 --> 00:01:44,596
Tell rejected (fetch first) from rejected
(non-fast-forward) and fix each.

26
00:01:44,936 --> 00:01:48,453
Push and delete branches and tags,
and predict which refs travel.

27
00:01:48,793 --> 00:01:52,967
Explain "Everything up-to-date" when
something you expected is not on the server.

28
00:01:53,307 --> 00:01:56,699
Explain why Git refuses a push
into a checked-out branch.

29
00:01:58,539 --> 00:02:02,903
In one sentence: git push 🟡 CAUTION asks a
remote to make some of its refs point at

30
00:02:02,943 --> 00:02:07,361
commits of yours, sends the objects it lacks,
and succeeds for each ref only if both

31
00:02:07,401 --> 00:02:09,590
your Git and the remote accept the update.

32
00:02:09,930 --> 00:02:13,828
Read that sentence again
for its three verbs. Asks.

33
00:02:13,868 --> 00:02:19,619
Sends. Succeeds only if both accept. A
push is a request, and there are two

34
00:02:19,659 --> 00:02:20,545
gatekeepers.

35
00:02:20,885 --> 00:02:25,810
A push runs in a fixed order, and you
will have the order on screen as today's

36
00:02:25,850 --> 00:02:29,121
diagram. In short: your Git
reads the server's refs.

37
00:02:29,161 --> 00:02:33,004
Your Git applies client rules
to each ref you want to update.

38
00:02:33,044 --> 00:02:38,160
If they fail, nothing is sent. If they
pass, your Git sends a line per ref, "old,

39
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:42,298
new, ref name", plus a packfile,
one file that holds the objects.

40
00:02:42,638 --> 00:02:47,315
The server puts the objects into a
quarantine directory, checks that the ref is

41
00:02:47,355 --> 00:02:52,332
still at the old value, applies its
receive.deny* settings and runs its hooks, which

42
00:02:52,372 --> 00:02:56,870
are scripts Git runs at a defined point.
If all of that passes, the refs are

43
00:02:56,910 --> 00:03:00,155
updated. Last, your Git moves
your remote-tracking ref.

44
00:03:00,495 --> 00:03:05,402
The client rules are in the manual's "push
rules": an existing branch may only be

45
00:03:05,442 --> 00:03:09,984
fast-forwarded, which means moved to a
descendant of the commit it has now.

46
00:03:10,024 --> 00:03:14,992
An existing tag may not be updated at all.
And creations and deletions are allowed

47
00:03:15,032 --> 00:03:20,062
unless configuration or hooks forbid them.
On screen is today's case, as your clone

48
00:03:20,102 --> 00:03:24,766
sees it: one commit of yours on top of
510ee94, where origin/main still sits.

49
00:03:24,806 --> 00:03:29,774
Whether pushing it is a fast-forward depends
on where the server's main really is.

50
00:03:30,114 --> 00:03:34,310
With arguments, each refspec is, optional
plus, source, colon, destination.

51
00:03:34,350 --> 00:03:38,941
The source is any commit expression of
yours. The destination is a ref name on the

52
00:03:38,981 --> 00:03:39,336
remote.

53
00:03:39,676 --> 00:03:44,688
Inside .git, in your repository, a push
changes only the remote-tracking ref of each

54
00:03:44,728 --> 00:03:49,439
ref that was pushed, and its reflog. On
the server: new objects and the updated

55
00:03:49,479 --> 00:03:49,740
refs.

56
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:59,033
Your branch, your index and
your working tree: unchanged.

57
00:03:59,073 --> 00:04:06,921
A push does not alter your
work. It alters theirs.

58
00:04:07,261 --> 00:04:10,482
And section 12.9 in two
sentences. A push moves refs.

59
00:04:10,522 --> 00:04:15,158
It checks nothing out. So if the pushed
ref is the branch that the receiving

60
00:04:15,198 --> 00:04:20,018
repository has checked out, that repository's
HEAD would name a commit that its

61
00:04:20,058 --> 00:04:24,817
index and working tree do not match, and
its next git status would present the

62
00:04:24,857 --> 00:04:29,063
pushed changes as local edits that
undo them. Git refuses by default.

63
00:04:30,903 --> 00:04:35,580
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
posting entries to a shared ledger.

64
00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:39,863
"I have seen everything up to entry
N; here are my entries after it."

65
00:04:39,903 --> 00:04:43,649
Your own clerk checks the claim
before you reach the counter.

66
00:04:43,689 --> 00:04:46,132
Then the office applies its house rules.

67
00:04:46,472 --> 00:04:50,178
Where it breaks: you can instruct
your clerk to skip the check.

68
00:04:50,218 --> 00:04:54,935
That is force, and it is the next video.
The office then refuses only if someone

69
00:04:54,975 --> 00:04:55,946
configured it to.

70
00:04:56,286 --> 00:04:59,551
Carry the two gatekeepers with
you. When the line says !

71
00:04:59,591 --> 00:05:03,269
[rejected], your clerk stopped
you, and your clone must change.

72
00:05:03,309 --> 00:05:06,279
When it says ! [remote
rejected], the office spoke.

73
00:05:06,319 --> 00:05:09,761
A policy on the server has to
be read, and its owner asked.

74
00:05:11,601 --> 00:05:19,223
Quick quiz, with the seven steps on
screen. Where does a line that says !

75
00:05:19,263 --> 00:05:24,577
[rejected] come from? A,
step 2, in your own clone.

76
00:05:24,617 --> 00:05:28,565
B, step 5, on the server. Your answer?

77
00:05:33,405 --> 00:05:37,459
A, step 2. Your own clerk said
no, and nothing was sent.

78
00:05:37,499 --> 00:05:41,845
Step 5 is the office, and its
line says ! [remote rejected].

79
00:05:43,685 --> 00:05:46,220
Part 1: one rule, two messages.

80
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:47,525
It says (fetch first).

81
00:05:47,865 --> 00:05:51,784
Your clone still shows origin/main on
510ee94. But the server's main names a commit

82
00:05:51,824 --> 00:05:55,457
that your repository does not contain,
so your Git cannot even test ancestry.

83
00:05:55,497 --> 00:05:59,273
The last status still shows the stale
[ahead 1], because a rejected push fetches

84
00:05:59,313 --> 00:05:59,654
nothing.

85
00:05:59,994 --> 00:06:04,467
Now fetch, and push again. Predict
the message. Say it out loud.

86
00:06:04,507 --> 00:06:05,172
I'll wait.

87
00:06:10,012 --> 00:06:14,097
This time: (non-fast-forward).

88
00:06:14,437 --> 00:06:17,891
After the fetch you have the
server's commit, 719650d.

89
00:06:17,931 --> 00:06:23,263
The ancestry test is possible, and it fails:
the server's tip is not an ancestor of

90
00:06:23,303 --> 00:06:27,534
what you push, so accepting it
would drop Asha's commit from main.

91
00:06:27,574 --> 00:06:32,452
One cause, two messages, and the only
difference is whether you had fetched.

92
00:06:32,792 --> 00:06:35,984
Now the word, and the answer
to the opening question.

93
00:06:36,024 --> 00:06:39,216
Both lines say ! [rejected],
not ! [remote rejected].

94
00:06:39,256 --> 00:06:43,912
Your own Git decided. Here is the proof,
the client side of the conversation.

95
00:06:44,252 --> 00:06:49,819
The server advertised its ref, the push<
line with the ID that starts 719650d.

96
00:06:49,859 --> 00:06:55,498
Your Git answered with an empty request,
push> 0000, and closed the connection.

97
00:06:55,538 --> 00:06:57,440
The server was never asked.

98
00:06:57,780 --> 00:07:09,812
The fix does not involve force. Try
it now. Thirty seconds, on paper.

99
00:07:09,852 --> 00:07:23,460
You are ahead 1 and behind 1. Write the
commands that get your commit onto the

100
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:28,884
server, then say them out loud.

101
00:07:33,724 --> 00:07:38,246
Integrate, then push. A rebase
here, and a merge works as well.

102
00:07:38,286 --> 00:07:39,911
Preview with --dry-run.

103
00:07:40,251 --> 00:07:45,106
The rebase re-created your commit
on top of 719650d, as 8bf53be.

104
00:07:45,146 --> 00:07:49,236
And look at the reflog of
origin/main: update by push.

105
00:07:49,276 --> 00:07:54,054
The successful push moved your
remote-tracking ref immediately.

106
00:07:54,094 --> 00:07:58,796
Your Git knows what the server
has now, so no fetch is needed.

107
00:07:59,136 --> 00:08:10,806
Part 2: pushes that fail before any
comparison. labs/run ch12/push-errors.

108
00:08:16,946 --> 00:08:24,092
src refspec <name> does not match any is
about your side: the source name matched no

109
00:08:24,132 --> 00:08:31,192
ref of yours. Either the name is wrong,
master where your branch is main, or half a

110
00:08:31,232 --> 00:08:38,293
branch name, or the repository has no commit
yet, because an unborn branch is not a

111
00:08:38,333 --> 00:08:45,051
ref. The textbook notes that this message
is among the thirty highest-voted Git

112
00:08:45,091 --> 00:08:52,066
questions on Stack Overflow. So if it has
stopped you before, you're in very large

113
00:08:52,106 --> 00:08:52,750
company.

114
00:08:53,090 --> 00:08:57,122
With a detached HEAD there is no current
branch whose upstream could supply a

115
00:08:57,162 --> 00:09:00,931
destination, so you name both sides.
The destination is written in full,

116
00:09:00,971 --> 00:09:05,215
refs/heads/..., because a detached HEAD
is not a ref under refs/heads/ from which

117
00:09:05,255 --> 00:09:07,277
Git could infer that you mean a branch.

118
00:09:07,617 --> 00:09:12,528
A misspelled remote name is taken for a
path to a repository, hence the misleading

119
00:09:12,568 --> 00:09:14,038
text about access rights.

120
00:09:14,378 --> 00:09:18,406
Part 3: deleting, tags, several
refs. labs/run ch12/push-refs.

121
00:09:18,746 --> 00:09:22,353
git push <remote> --delete <branch>
🔴 DANGEROUS. The five answers.

122
00:09:22,393 --> 00:09:26,773
It changes one ref on the server. It can
destroy the only name that the branch's

123
00:09:26,813 --> 00:09:30,641
commits have there, and a bare server
keeps no record of the deletion.

124
00:09:30,681 --> 00:09:35,116
Preview with git log --oneline origin/<branch>
--not origin/main, which lists the

125
00:09:35,156 --> 00:09:39,758
commits that only that branch holds. Recover
by pushing the tip again from any clone

126
00:09:39,798 --> 00:09:43,957
that has it. It is appropriate for
branches that are merged, or abandoned by

127
00:09:43,997 --> 00:09:44,509
agreement.

128
00:09:44,849 --> 00:09:53,778
The branch is gone on the server
and so is your remote-tracking ref.

129
00:09:53,818 --> 00:10:01,033
Your local branch stays, with
its upstream marked gone.

130
00:10:01,373 --> 00:10:05,265
Tags. An annotated tag is an
object of its own, with a message.

131
00:10:05,305 --> 00:10:10,071
A lightweight tag is only a name. You
create one of each and run a bare push.

132
00:10:10,111 --> 00:10:11,257
Predict the output.

133
00:10:16,097 --> 00:10:19,128
"Everything up-to-date",
and the server has no tags.

134
00:10:19,168 --> 00:10:23,736
That phrase means: for the refs this push
selected, there was nothing to send.

135
00:10:23,776 --> 00:10:27,221
It does not mean that everything
you made is on the server.

136
00:10:27,261 --> 00:10:28,875
Tags travel only when asked.

137
00:10:29,215 --> 00:10:34,768
git push origin <tag> pushes one. --follow-tags
adds annotated tags that point into

138
00:10:34,808 --> 00:10:39,552
the commits being pushed: v0.2.1 went,
the lightweight nightly did not.

139
00:10:39,592 --> 00:10:41,304
--tags pushes all of them.

140
00:10:41,644 --> 00:10:45,526
That is the tag rule: a tag that exists on
the remote is not replaced without force.

141
00:10:45,866 --> 00:10:52,629
Now two refs in one push. main is behind
the server, and release/0.1 is one commit

142
00:10:52,669 --> 00:10:57,938
ahead. First with --atomic. Predict
what happens to release/0.1.

143
00:11:02,778 --> 00:11:06,972
Nothing was sent, because one
ref failed. Without --atomic:

144
00:11:07,312 --> 00:11:13,906
release/0.1 was updated although main
was rejected, and the exit status is 1.

145
00:11:13,946 --> 00:11:20,970
Refs in one push are independent by default.
A release script that pushes a branch

146
00:11:21,010 --> 00:11:25,795
and a tag needs --atomic and
must check the exit status.

147
00:11:26,135 --> 00:11:30,226
Part 4: the server's own rules.
labs/run ch12/server-rules.

148
00:11:30,266 --> 00:11:36,108
The demo uses --force here only to get past
your own clerk and let the office speak.

149
00:11:36,148 --> 00:11:38,279
Force itself is the next video.

150
00:11:38,619 --> 00:11:48,054
Read the line: ! [remote rejected]. And
above it a remote: line carrying the

151
00:11:48,094 --> 00:11:57,654
server's own words. --force switches off
your Git's checks, not the server's.

152
00:12:03,494 --> 00:12:08,403
A pre-receive hook reads one "old new ref"
line per proposed update and can refuse

153
00:12:08,443 --> 00:12:12,025
the whole push, while the pushed
objects wait in quarantine.

154
00:12:12,065 --> 00:12:16,672
Here it refuses every direct update of
main, and its message reaches you on a

155
00:12:16,712 --> 00:12:17,457
remote: line.

156
00:12:17,797 --> 00:12:21,268
Part 5: a repository with a working
tree. labs/run ch12/push-non-bare.

157
00:12:21,308 --> 00:12:24,027
staging-box is an ordinary
clone with main checked out.

158
00:12:24,067 --> 00:12:24,428
Predict.

159
00:12:29,268 --> 00:12:33,810
"Refusing to update checked out branch."
receive.denyCurrentBranch defaults to

160
00:12:33,850 --> 00:12:37,335
refuse. Any branch that is not
checked out can be pushed to.

161
00:12:37,675 --> 00:12:42,663
For the legitimate case, a test or deployment
machine that you reach over SSH, the

162
00:12:42,703 --> 00:12:47,691
receiving side can opt in to updateInstead,
which updates the working tree as part

163
00:12:47,731 --> 00:12:48,427
of the push.

164
00:12:53,967 --> 00:12:59,016
It works only while the target is clean.
One uncommitted edit on the box makes every

165
00:12:59,056 --> 00:13:04,043
later push fail with "Working directory has
unstaged changes", which is the correct

166
00:13:04,083 --> 00:13:08,647
outcome: somebody hot-fixed on the
machine, and the push tells you before it

167
00:13:08,687 --> 00:13:13,553
overwrites anything. The general rule
stands: a repository that people push to is

168
00:13:13,593 --> 00:13:13,856
bare.

169
00:13:15,696 --> 00:13:17,344
Five mistakes to watch for.

170
00:13:17,684 --> 00:13:22,544
Answering a rejection with --force. Root
cause: rejected means the server's tip is

171
00:13:22,584 --> 00:13:27,444
not an ancestor of yours, so forcing would
drop the other person's commit; the fix

172
00:13:27,484 --> 00:13:28,997
is to fetch and integrate.

173
00:13:29,337 --> 00:13:32,978
Not reading whether the line says
rejected or remote rejected.

174
00:13:33,018 --> 00:13:37,668
Root cause: they come from different
gatekeepers, your own Git at step 2 or the

175
00:13:37,708 --> 00:13:40,458
server at step 5, and
need different responses.

176
00:13:40,798 --> 00:13:44,452
Assuming a release tag was
pushed because the branch was.

177
00:13:44,492 --> 00:13:49,441
Root cause: tags are not pushed unless
named, or selected by --follow-tags or

178
00:13:49,481 --> 00:13:49,895
--tags.

179
00:13:50,235 --> 00:13:54,674
Pushing a branch and a tag in one
command and trusting partial success.

180
00:13:54,714 --> 00:13:59,910
Root cause: refs in one push are independent
unless --atomic is given, and the exit

181
00:13:59,950 --> 00:14:01,361
status is 1 either way.

182
00:14:01,701 --> 00:14:05,757
Typing git push origin master in a
repository whose branch is main.

183
00:14:05,797 --> 00:14:10,770
Root cause: src refspec does not match any
means the source name matched no ref on

184
00:14:10,810 --> 00:14:11,382
your side.

185
00:14:13,222 --> 00:14:18,491
Now, out of the lab. An on-call engineer
for a model-serving team tries to push a

186
00:14:18,531 --> 00:14:23,800
hotfix and reports: "it said rejected".
The textbook's reply is the title of this

187
00:14:23,840 --> 00:14:26,881
slide. Read the word
before the bracket closes.

188
00:14:27,221 --> 00:14:30,914
rejected means your clone must
change: fetch and integrate.

189
00:14:30,954 --> 00:14:36,166
remote rejected means a policy on the server
spoke: read the remote: lines and talk

190
00:14:36,206 --> 00:14:40,406
to whoever owns that policy. Retrying
with --force answers neither.

191
00:14:40,746 --> 00:14:48,042
On GitHub the server-side rules are rulesets,
branch protection and push protection.

192
00:14:48,082 --> 00:14:55,116
They answer in the same shape. GitHub's
documentation gives remote: error: GH006:

193
00:14:55,156 --> 00:14:59,570
Protected branch update
failed for refs/heads/main.

194
00:14:59,610 --> 00:15:05,247
as the reply of a branch protection
rule. Chapter 18 covers them.

195
00:15:05,287 --> 00:15:11,098
This is described from the documentation
and was not captured here.

196
00:15:11,438 --> 00:15:16,164
So the skill transfers. On a plain
Git server the office's rules are

197
00:15:16,204 --> 00:15:20,299
receive.denyNonFastForwards,
receive.denyDeletes and hooks.

198
00:15:20,339 --> 00:15:25,905
On GitHub they have other names and more
features. In both, the reply arrives on

199
00:15:25,945 --> 00:15:29,760
remote: lines and the status
line says remote rejected.

200
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:39,501
Your turn. Do Lab 7.2, "A rejected push,
then fetch and integrate by merge and by

201
00:15:39,541 --> 00:15:43,227
rebase", in lab-manual/m07-remotes.md.

202
00:15:43,567 --> 00:15:45,097
Predict before each command:

203
00:15:45,437 --> 00:15:49,860
Which of the two rejection messages will
the first push print, and why that one?

204
00:15:50,200 --> 00:15:55,244
After the fetch, what will the second push
print, and what does git status -sb show?

205
00:15:55,584 --> 00:16:00,593
After integrating by merge and then again
by rebase: what does the graph look like,

206
00:16:00,633 --> 00:16:05,643
and what is the top entry of git reflog
show origin/main after the successful push?

207
00:16:05,983 --> 00:16:10,889
The challenge is Exercise 7.10, Level 5,
"the hotfix that was pushed and is not on

208
00:16:10,929 --> 00:16:13,845
the server", in
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

209
00:16:15,685 --> 00:16:20,950
Q206: "Two engineers in the same situation
got rejected (fetch first) and rejected

210
00:16:20,990 --> 00:16:25,479
(non-fast-forward). Explain the
difference and who made each decision.

211
00:16:25,519 --> 00:16:27,678
How is remote rejected different?"

212
00:16:30,018 --> 00:16:34,942
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
places all three messages on the steps of a

213
00:16:34,982 --> 00:16:39,725
push and says, for each, which side had
the information and which side decided.

214
00:16:39,765 --> 00:16:44,810
It explains the two client messages as two
states of knowledge about the same graph,

215
00:16:44,850 --> 00:16:48,866
and it can say what evidence shows
that the server was never asked.

216
00:16:49,206 --> 00:16:53,436
For remote rejected it names at least two
things on a server that can produce it and

217
00:16:53,476 --> 00:16:57,603
says where the server's explanation appears.
It ends with what the engineer should

218
00:16:57,643 --> 00:16:59,941
do in each case, and
force is in none of them.

219
00:17:01,781 --> 00:17:04,708
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

220
00:17:05,048 --> 00:17:09,758
A push is a request: my Git checks the
client rules first, and the receiving

221
00:17:09,798 --> 00:17:12,508
repository applies its
own rules after that.

222
00:17:12,848 --> 00:17:17,924
rejected (fetch first) and rejected
(non-fast-forward) are the same refusal by my

223
00:17:17,964 --> 00:17:23,041
own Git, before and after I have fetched.
The fix is to integrate and push again.

224
00:17:23,381 --> 00:17:26,868
remote rejected is the server's
policy speaking on remote: lines.

225
00:17:26,908 --> 00:17:31,155
Tags are pushed only when named, refs in
one push are independent unless I pass

226
00:17:31,195 --> 00:17:35,713
--atomic, and "Everything up-to-date" speaks
only about the refs that push selected.

227
00:17:35,753 --> 00:17:40,054
A push moves refs and checks nothing out,
so Git refuses to update a checked-out

228
00:17:40,094 --> 00:17:41,791
branch in a non-bare repository.

229
00:17:43,631 --> 00:17:47,028
Read sections 12.7 and 12.9 of Chapter 12.

230
00:17:47,368 --> 00:17:53,345
Do Exercise 7.3, Level 1, "publish a
branch, then delete it on the server", and

231
00:17:53,385 --> 00:17:58,524
Exercise 7.8, Level 3, "a push that
the other side refuses", both in

232
00:17:58,564 --> 00:18:01,038
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

233
00:18:01,378 --> 00:18:07,540
Today you read a push as a request with
two gatekeepers, and you can tell from one

234
00:18:07,580 --> 00:18:12,002
word which of them said no. Do
Lab 7.2 while this is fresh.

235
00:18:12,042 --> 00:18:16,464
Next time: forcing a push, what
it destroys, and the lease.

236
00:18:16,504 --> 00:18:20,397
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

237
00:18:20,437 --> 00:18:22,212
See you in the next one.
